<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Banu Qasi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th–10th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Banu Qasi were a Muslim dynasty of Hispano-Roman or Visigothic origin who ruled much of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> valley from the eighth to the early tenth century, descended according to tradition from a count Cassius who converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> after the Arab conquest. Based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tudela">Tudela</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> and other towns of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-march">Upper March</a>, they alternated between loyalty and revolt towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> and allied with the Arista rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/banu-qasi.jpg" alt="Banu Qasi" width="320" height="260" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sugaar at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdom_of_Pamplona_(c._925).gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
